Large language models (LLMs) have shown excellent performance across a wide range of tasks.
Prefilling is a crucial phase in LLM inference, which determines the response latency (measured as the time to first token, TTFT) .
Quantization has demonstrated potential for deploying LLMs on resource-constrained devices.
Post-training quantization (PTQ) is widely used since it does not require training or fine-tuning LLMs.
Existing PTQ methods use predetermined quantization strategies for the entire LLM inference.
It is inefficient for dynamic workloads in prefilling.

Existing PTQ methods have three representative quantization strategies.
SmoothQuant focuses on W8A8.
It compresses weights and activations to INT8 in order to leverage integer computation on hardware.
This method is friendly to compute-bound scenarios.
AWQ and MARLIN , marlin study on W4A16.
They use 4-bit weights and 16-bit activations.
This reduces the memory overhead and thus benefits memory-bound scenarios.
Qserve and QQQ concentrate on W4A8.
They quantize weights to 4-bit and activations to 8-bit for both compute- and memory-bound scenarios.

These works overlook a basic fact: LLM inference is dynamic, especially in prefilling.
In the prefill phase, LLM processes the entire input sequence.
The computation workload of linear layers shifts with the input sequence length. 
Short sequences are memory-bound and it is prefer to lower weight precision such as W4A16.
Long sequences are compute-bound, favoring lower computation precision such as W8A8.
In decoding, LLM generates tokens auto-regressively with a fixed sequence length.
However, in these works, the linear layer is locked into a predetermined quantization strategy, which is inefficiency for dynamic workloads in prefilling.

In this paper, we propose Kairos, a dynamic quantization framework for LLM prefilling.
Our key idea is to decouple storage and computation.
For storage, a 4-bit quantization of weights is used to reduce memory overhead.
For computation, different quantization strategies are used to accelerate the prefilling at runtime.
First, we propose a two-stage quantization algorithm.
It allows the weights of linear layers to be dequantized to 8-bit or 16-bit.
Then, we present a hardware-aware performance profiler.
It is used to explore the optimal quantization strategy for each linear layer under different input sequences.
Finally, we design a runtime precision converter.
it converts the precisions of weights and activations such that each linear layer uses the optimal quantization strategy according to the input sequence length in prefilling.